// The x87 80-bit extended `long double`, on words. A value lives in 16 bytes
// of the store: the 64-bit significand (its integer bit explicit) at +0, then
// 16 bits holding the sign (bit 15) and the 15-bit biased exponent at +8, six
// bytes of zero after. Everything here is exact: sums, products and quotients
// are formed with their full width and rounded once, to nearest even, at 64
// bits, as the x87 does.
//
// Word primitives first. ld_clz counts the leading zero bits of a non-zero
// word (a word with the top bit set answers 0); ld_mulhi is the high 64 bits of
// the 128-bit product of two unsigned words, from 32-bit limbs.
ld_clz(word lxv) {
  word lxn = 0;
  while(lxv > 0) {
    lxv = lxv << 1;
    lxn = lxn + 1;
  }
} return word lxn;
